WEBVTT

1
00:00:04.520 --> 00:00:07.398
One of the most common,
complex applications that

2
00:00:07.398 --> 00:00:11.715
people are building using an LLM
is a system that can answer

3
00:00:11.715 --> 00:00:14.593
questions on top of or about
a document.

4
00:00:15.154 --> 00:00:18.462
So, given a piece of text,
maybe extracted from a

5
00:00:18.462 --> 00:00:21.771
PDF file or from a webpage or from
some company's

6
00:00:21.771 --> 00:00:25.410
intranet internal document collection, can
you use an LLM to answer

7
00:00:25.410 --> 00:00:28.388
questions about the
content of those documents to help

8
00:00:28.388 --> 00:00:30.373
users gain a
deeper understanding and

9
00:00:30.373 --> 00:00:33.020
get access to the information that
they need?

10
00:00:33.020 --> 00:00:35.802
This is really powerful because it
starts to combine

11
00:00:35.802 --> 00:00:38.274
these language models
with data that they weren't

12
00:00:38.274 --> 00:00:40.747
originally trained on. So
it makes them much

13
00:00:40.747 --> 00:00:43.839
more flexible and adaptable
to your use case. It's also

14
00:00:43.839 --> 00:00:47.857
really exciting because we'll start to
move beyond language models, prompts, and output

15
00:00:47.857 --> 00:00:50.948
parsers and start introducing
some more of the key components

16
00:00:50.948 --> 00:00:53.730
of LangChain, such as embedding
models and vector stores.

17
00:00:53.730 --> 00:00:53.730


18
00:00:54.532 --> 00:00:56.085
As Andrew mentioned,
this is one of the

19
00:00:56.085 --> 00:00:58.220
more popular chains that we've got, so
I hope you're excited.

20
00:00:58.220 --> 00:01:00.277
In fact,
embeddings and vector stores

21
00:01:00.277 --> 00:01:03.020
are some of the
most powerful modern techniques,

22
00:01:03.020 --> 00:01:07.820
so if you have not seen them yet,
they are very much worth learning

23
00:01:07.820 --> 00:01:10.220
about. So with that, let's dive in!

24
00:01:10.220 --> 00:01:13.154
Let's do it! So we're going
to start by importing

25
00:01:13.154 --> 00:01:15.207
the environment variables as
we always do.

26
00:01:16.249 --> 00:01:18.757
Now we're going to import some
things that will help

27
00:01:18.757 --> 00:01:21.516
us when building this chain. We're
going to import the retrieval

28
00:01:21.516 --> 00:01:24.525
QA chain. This will do retrieval over
some documents. We're going to

29
00:01:24.525 --> 00:01:27.033
import our favorite chat open
AI language model. We're going

30
00:01:27.033 --> 00:01:29.290
to import
a document loader. This is going to

31
00:01:29.290 --> 00:01:32.550
be used to load some proprietary data that
we're going to combine with

32
00:01:32.550 --> 00:01:34.556
the language model. In
this case it's going

33
00:01:34.556 --> 00:01:35.810
to be in a
CSV.

34
00:01:36.331 --> 00:01:38.687
So we're
going to import the CSV loader. Finally

35
00:01:38.687 --> 00:01:40.519
we're going
to import a vector store.

36
00:01:41.121 --> 00:01:43.763
There are many different types
of vector stores and we'll

37
00:01:43.763 --> 00:01:46.406
cover what exactly these are
later on but we're going

38
00:01:46.406 --> 00:01:48.520
to get started with the "DocArrayInMemorySearch" vector
store.

39
00:01:48.520 --> 00:01:50.634
This is
really nice because it's an in-memory

40
00:01:50.634 --> 00:01:53.013
vector store and
it doesn't require connecting to an

41
00:01:53.013 --> 00:01:55.127
external database of any
kind so it makes

42
00:01:55.127 --> 00:01:56.712
it really easy to get started.


43
00:01:57.494 --> 00:02:01.043
We're also going to import display and
markdown to common

44
00:02:01.043 --> 00:02:03.172
utilities for displaying
information in Jupyter

45
00:02:03.172 --> 00:02:03.526
notebooks.

46
00:02:04.408 --> 00:02:07.785
We've provided a CSV of
outdoor clothing that we're going

47
00:02:07.785 --> 00:02:11.162
to use to combine with
the language model. Here we're

48
00:02:11.162 --> 00:02:13.863
going to
initialize a loader, the CSV loader,

49
00:02:13.863 --> 00:02:15.890
with a path
to this file.

50
00:02:17.420 --> 00:02:21.478
We're next going to import an index, the
"VectorStoreIndexCreator". This

51
00:02:21.478 --> 00:02:25.132
will help us create a
vector store really easily.

52
00:02:25.933 --> 00:02:28.965
As we can see below,
there will only be a few

53
00:02:28.965 --> 00:02:30.620
lines of code to create this.

54
00:02:33.800 --> 00:02:36.686
To create it, we're
going to specify two things.

55
00:02:37.408 --> 00:02:39.995
First, we're going to specify the
vector store class.

56
00:02:40.537 --> 00:02:43.019
As mentioned before, we're
going to use this vector store, as

57
00:02:43.019 --> 00:02:45.050
it's a particularly easy one to
get started with.

58
00:02:45.973 --> 00:02:49.696
After it's been created, we're then
going to call "from_loaders", which takes in

59
00:02:49.696 --> 00:02:51.128
a list of document
loaders.

60
00:02:51.770 --> 00:02:54.775
We've only got one loader that
we really care about, so that's what

61
00:02:54.775 --> 00:02:55.700
we're passing in here.

62
00:02:57.800 --> 00:02:59.990
It's now been created and
we can start to

63
00:02:59.990 --> 00:03:00.964
ask questions about it.

64
00:03:02.066 --> 00:03:05.360
Below we'll cover what exactly happened
under the hood, so let's not

65
00:03:05.360 --> 00:03:08.380
worry about that for now. Here, we'll
start with a query.

66
00:03:09.061 --> 00:03:15.215
We'll then create a response using "index.query"
and pass in this query.

67
00:03:15.215 --> 00:03:15.215


68
00:03:16.858 --> 00:03:20.245
Again, we'll cover what's going
on under the hood down below.

69
00:03:20.245 --> 00:03:20.245


70
00:03:20.847 --> 00:03:23.250
For now, we'll
just wait for it to respond.

71
00:03:29.240 --> 00:03:33.846
After it finishes, we can now take a
look at what exactly was returned.

72
00:03:34.467 --> 00:03:37.639
We've gotten back a table
in markdown with names

73
00:03:37.639 --> 00:03:40.459
and descriptions for all
shirts with sun protection.

74
00:03:41.321 --> 00:03:43.253
We've also got a nice
little summary that the

75
00:03:43.253 --> 00:03:44.327
language model has provided us.

76
00:03:45.509 --> 00:03:48.682
So we've gone over how to do question
answering over your

77
00:03:48.682 --> 00:03:52.144
documents, but what exactly is going
on underneath the hood? First, let's

78
00:03:52.144 --> 00:03:55.605
think about the general idea. We want
to use language models and

79
00:03:55.605 --> 00:03:57.913
combine it with a lot
of our documents.

80
00:03:58.595 --> 00:03:59.156
But there's a key issue.

81
00:03:59.657 --> 00:04:01.701
Language models can only
inspect a few thousand

82
00:04:01.701 --> 00:04:02.723
words at a time.

83
00:04:03.364 --> 00:04:06.260
So if we have really large documents,
how can we get

84
00:04:06.260 --> 00:04:08.366
the language model to
answer questions about everything

85
00:04:08.366 --> 00:04:09.155
that's in there?

86
00:04:10.177 --> 00:04:14.630
This is where embeddings and
vector stores come into play. First,

87
00:04:14.630 --> 00:04:16.249
let's talk about embeddings.

88
00:04:17.431 --> 00:04:19.115
Embeddings create
numerical representations

89
00:04:19.115 --> 00:04:20.798
for pieces of text.

90
00:04:21.640 --> 00:04:23.450
This numerical representation
captures the semantic

91
00:04:23.450 --> 00:04:26.770
meaning of the piece of text
that it's been run over.

92
00:04:27.772 --> 00:04:30.778
Pieces of text with similar content
will have similar vectors.

93
00:04:31.840 --> 00:04:34.585
This lets us compare pieces
of text in the vector space.

94
00:04:35.226 --> 00:04:37.016
In the example below,
we can see that

95
00:04:37.016 --> 00:04:37.912
we have three sentences.

96
00:04:38.914 --> 00:04:42.080
The first two are about pets,
while the third is about a car.

97
00:04:43.182 --> 00:04:46.200
If we look at the
representation in the numeric space,

98
00:04:46.200 --> 00:04:49.821
we can see that when we compare the
two vectors on the

99
00:04:49.821 --> 00:04:52.838
pieces of text corresponding
to the sentences about pets, they're

100
00:04:52.838 --> 00:04:53.442
very similar.

101
00:04:53.943 --> 00:04:56.288
While if we compare it to
the one that talks about a car,

102
00:04:56.288 --> 00:04:57.189
they're not similar at all.

103
00:04:58.191 --> 00:05:00.801
This will let us easily
figure out which pieces of

104
00:05:00.801 --> 00:05:03.672
text are like each other,
which will be very useful as

105
00:05:03.672 --> 00:05:06.804
we think about which pieces of
text we want to include when

106
00:05:06.804 --> 00:05:09.153
passing to the language model to
answer a question.

107
00:05:09.874 --> 00:05:11.918
The next component that we're
going to cover is

108
00:05:11.918 --> 00:05:12.599
the vector database.

109
00:05:13.100 --> 00:05:15.606
A vector database is a way
to store these

110
00:05:15.606 --> 00:05:18.112
vector representations that
we created in the previous step.

111
00:05:18.112 --> 00:05:20.340
The way that we
create this vector database

112
00:05:20.340 --> 00:05:22.567
is we populate it
with chunks of text

113
00:05:22.567 --> 00:05:23.681
coming from incoming documents.

114
00:05:24.322 --> 00:05:27.234
When we get a big incoming document,
we're first going to break it

115
00:05:27.234 --> 00:05:28.130
up into smaller chunks.

116
00:05:28.931 --> 00:05:31.049
This helps create pieces
of text that are

117
00:05:31.049 --> 00:05:33.432
smaller than the original document,
which is useful because

118
00:05:33.432 --> 00:05:36.609
we may not be able to pass the
whole document to the

119
00:05:36.609 --> 00:05:39.257
language model. So we want to
create these small chunks

120
00:05:39.257 --> 00:05:41.375
so we can only
pass the most relevant

121
00:05:41.375 --> 00:05:42.698
ones to the language model.

122
00:05:43.260 --> 00:05:46.080
We then create an embedding
for each of these chunks,

123
00:05:46.080 --> 00:05:48.900
and then we store those in a vector
database. That's

124
00:05:48.900 --> 00:05:50.874
what happens when we create the index.

125
00:05:51.596 --> 00:05:54.406
Now that we've got this index, we
can use it during

126
00:05:54.406 --> 00:05:56.450
runtime to find the
pieces of text most

127
00:05:56.450 --> 00:05:57.728
relevant to an incoming query.

128
00:05:58.349 --> 00:06:00.499
When a query comes in,
we first create an

129
00:06:00.499 --> 00:06:01.455
embedding for that query.

130
00:06:02.177 --> 00:06:04.235
We then compare it
to all the vectors

131
00:06:04.235 --> 00:06:07.066
in the vector database,
and we pick the n most similar.

132
00:06:07.888 --> 00:06:11.034
These are then returned,
and we can pass those in the prompt

133
00:06:11.034 --> 00:06:14.181
to the language model to get back a
final answer. So above,

134
00:06:14.181 --> 00:06:17.066
we created this chain and
only a few lines of code.

135
00:06:17.607 --> 00:06:19.150
That's
great for getting started quickly.

136
00:06:19.671 --> 00:06:23.925
But let's now do it a bit more step-by-step and
understand what exactly is going on

137
00:06:23.925 --> 00:06:26.584
under the hood. The first step
is similar to above.

138
00:06:27.165 --> 00:06:29.742
We're
going to create a document loader, loading

139
00:06:29.742 --> 00:06:32.641
from that CSV with all the
descriptions of the

140
00:06:32.641 --> 00:06:35.540
products that we want to do
question answering over.

141
00:06:36.140 --> 00:06:40.340
We can then load
documents from this document loader.

142
00:06:40.340 --> 00:06:40.340


143
00:06:40.940 --> 00:06:45.521
If we look at the individual documents,
we can see that each

144
00:06:45.521 --> 00:06:49.340
document corresponds to one of
the products in the CSV.

145
00:06:49.940 --> 00:06:52.323
Previously,
we talked about creating chunks.

146
00:06:52.865 --> 00:06:54.391
Because these documents
are already so small,

147
00:06:54.391 --> 00:06:56.354
we actually don't
need to do any chunking here.

148
00:06:56.875 --> 00:06:59.810
And so we can create embeddings
directly.

149
00:07:01.106 --> 00:07:04.029
To create embeddings, we're
going to use OpenAI's embedding

150
00:07:04.029 --> 00:07:04.354
class.

151
00:07:05.216 --> 00:07:07.221
We can import it and initialize it here.

152
00:07:08.224 --> 00:07:10.618
If we want to
see what these embeddings do,

153
00:07:10.618 --> 00:07:13.810
we can actually take a look at what
happens when we embed

154
00:07:13.810 --> 00:07:15.140
a particular piece of text.

155
00:07:20.840 --> 00:07:24.191
Let's use the "embed_query" method on
the embeddings object to create an

156
00:07:24.191 --> 00:07:26.705
embeddings for a
particular piece of text. In this

157
00:07:26.705 --> 00:07:28.940
case, the sentence, "Hi, my name
is Harrison."

158
00:07:31.480 --> 00:07:34.795
If we take a look at this embedding,
we can see that there are

159
00:07:34.795 --> 00:07:35.980
over a thousand different elements.

160
00:07:40.920 --> 00:07:44.126
Each of these elements
is a different numerical value.

161
00:07:44.728 --> 00:07:47.581
Combined, this creates the
overall numerical representation

162
00:07:47.581 --> 00:07:49.620
for this piece of text.

163
00:07:50.820 --> 00:07:52.852
We want to create
embeddings for all the

164
00:07:52.852 --> 00:07:54.885
pieces of text that
we just loaddand then

165
00:07:54.885 --> 00:07:57.425
we also want to store them in a
vector store.

166
00:07:58.467 --> 00:08:01.531
We can do that by using the "from_documents"
method

167
00:08:01.531 --> 00:08:02.892
on the vector store.

168
00:08:03.713 --> 00:08:06.621
This method takes in
a list of documents,

169
00:08:06.621 --> 00:08:10.620
an embedding object, and then we'll
create an overall vector store.

170
00:08:11.820 --> 00:08:15.278
We can now use this vector store to
find pieces of text

171
00:08:15.278 --> 00:08:16.719
similar to an incoming query.

172
00:08:17.964 --> 00:08:22.020
So let's look at the query, "Please
suggest a shirt with sunblocking".

173
00:08:22.020 --> 00:08:22.020


174
00:08:22.620 --> 00:08:25.120
If we use the similarity
search method on the vector

175
00:08:25.120 --> 00:08:28.620
store and pass in a query,
we will get back a list of documents.

176
00:08:28.620 --> 00:08:28.620


177
00:08:36.520 --> 00:08:39.583
We can see that
it returns four documents,

178
00:08:39.583 --> 00:08:44.562
and if we look at the first one,
we can see that it

179
00:08:44.562 --> 00:08:46.860
is indeed a shirt about sunblocking.


180
00:08:47.460 --> 00:08:50.450
So, how do we use
this to do question

181
00:08:50.450 --> 00:08:54.105
answering over our own documents?
First, we need to create a

182
00:08:54.105 --> 00:08:57.095
retriever from this vector store.
A retriever is a

183
00:08:57.095 --> 00:08:59.753
generic interface that can
be underpinned by any

184
00:08:59.753 --> 00:09:02.744
method that takes in
a query and returns documents.

185
00:09:02.744 --> 00:09:06.398
Vector stores and embeddings are
one such method to do so,

186
00:09:06.398 --> 00:09:08.724
although there are
plenty of different methods,

187
00:09:08.724 --> 00:09:10.718
some less advanced,
some more advanced.

188
00:09:11.640 --> 00:09:14.380
Next, because we want to
do text generation and

189
00:09:14.380 --> 00:09:17.424
return a natural language response,
we're going to import a

190
00:09:17.424 --> 00:09:19.860
language model and we're going
to use ChatOpenAI.

191
00:09:20.460 --> 00:09:24.373
If we were doing this by hand,
what we would do is

192
00:09:24.373 --> 00:09:27.960
we would combine the documents
into a single piece of text.

193
00:09:28.763 --> 00:09:32.693
So we'd do something like this, where
we join all the

194
00:09:32.693 --> 00:09:35.551
page content in the
documents into a variable

195
00:09:35.551 --> 00:09:38.409
and then would pass
this variable or a

196
00:09:38.409 --> 00:09:41.981
variant on the question, like, "Please
list all your shirts

197
00:09:41.981 --> 00:09:44.839
with sun protection in
a table in markdown

198
00:09:44.839 --> 00:09:47.697
and summarize each one." into
the language model.

199
00:09:48.919 --> 00:09:53.262
And if we print out the response here,
we can see that we get back a

200
00:09:53.262 --> 00:09:54.890
table exactly as we asked for.

201
00:09:55.712 --> 00:09:58.532
All of those steps can be
encapsulated with the

202
00:09:58.532 --> 00:09:59.158
LangChain chain.

203
00:09:59.839 --> 00:10:02.224
So here we can
create a retrieval QA chain.

204
00:10:02.885 --> 00:10:05.357
This does retrieval and then does
question answering over

205
00:10:05.357 --> 00:10:07.830
the retrieved documents.
To create such a chain, we'll

206
00:10:07.830 --> 00:10:10.302
pass in a few different things. First,
we'll pass

207
00:10:10.302 --> 00:10:11.401
in the language model.

208
00:10:12.122 --> 00:10:14.707
This will be used for doing
the text generation at the end.

209
00:10:15.688 --> 00:10:19.426
Next, we'll pass in the chain type. We're
going to use "stuff". This is

210
00:10:19.426 --> 00:10:21.562
the simplest method as
it just stuffs all

211
00:10:21.562 --> 00:10:23.697
the documents into context
and makes one call

212
00:10:23.697 --> 00:10:24.765
to a language model.

213
00:10:25.286 --> 00:10:27.290
There are a few other
methods that you can use

214
00:10:27.290 --> 00:10:29.894
to do question answering that I'll
maybe touch on at the end, but

215
00:10:29.894 --> 00:10:31.497
we're not
going to look at in detail.

216
00:10:32.159 --> 00:10:33.681
Third, we're
going to pass in a retriever.

217
00:10:34.423 --> 00:10:36.814
The retriever we created
above is just an

218
00:10:36.814 --> 00:10:38.009
interface for fetching documents.

219
00:10:38.590 --> 00:10:40.354
This will be used to
fetch the documents and pass

220
00:10:40.354 --> 00:10:41.235
it to the language model.

221
00:10:41.776 --> 00:10:44.860
And then finally, we're
going to set "verbose=True".

222
00:10:45.460 --> 00:10:50.616
Now, we can create a
query and we can run the chain on

223
00:10:50.616 --> 00:10:51.410
this query.

224
00:11:08.040 --> 00:11:11.649
When we get the response,
we can again display it using

225
00:11:11.649 --> 00:11:14.930
the display and markdown utilities.
You can pause the video

226
00:11:14.930 --> 00:11:18.540
here and try it out
with a bunch of different queries.

227
00:11:20.520 --> 00:11:23.143
So that's how you do it in detail, but
remember that we can still

228
00:11:23.143 --> 00:11:25.016
do it pretty easily with
just the one line that

229
00:11:25.016 --> 00:11:25.766
we had up above.

230
00:11:26.507 --> 00:11:29.672
So, these two things equate
to the same result.

231
00:11:30.193 --> 00:11:32.409
And that's part of the interesting
stuff about LangChain. You

232
00:11:32.409 --> 00:11:34.847
can do it in one line, or
you can look at

233
00:11:34.847 --> 00:11:36.619
the individual things and
break it down into

234
00:11:36.619 --> 00:11:37.506
five more detailed ones.

235
00:11:38.127 --> 00:11:40.147
The five more detailed
ones let you set

236
00:11:40.147 --> 00:11:42.926
more specifics about what
exactly is going on, but the one-liner

237
00:11:42.926 --> 00:11:46.462
is easy to get started. So
up to you as to how you'd prefer

238
00:11:46.462 --> 00:11:47.220
to go forward.

239
00:11:47.820 --> 00:11:51.011
We can also customize the index when we're
creating it. And

240
00:11:51.011 --> 00:11:54.202
so if you remember, when
we created it by hand, we

241
00:11:54.202 --> 00:11:56.523
specified an embedding. And
we can specify an

242
00:11:56.523 --> 00:11:59.714
embedding here as well. And
so this will give us flexibility

243
00:11:59.714 --> 00:12:01.455
over how the
embeddings themselves are

244
00:12:01.455 --> 00:12:04.356
created. And we can also swap
out the vector store

245
00:12:04.356 --> 00:12:06.967
here for a different
type of vector store. So

246
00:12:06.967 --> 00:12:10.449
there's the same level of customization that
you did when you create

247
00:12:10.449 --> 00:12:13.350
it by hand that's also available
when you create the

248
00:12:13.350 --> 00:12:13.930
index here.

249
00:12:15.073 --> 00:12:17.042
We use the "stuff method"
in this notebook.

250
00:12:17.042 --> 00:12:19.011
The stuff method is
really nice because it's

251
00:12:19.011 --> 00:12:22.703
pretty simple. You just put all of
it into one prompt and send that to

252
00:12:22.703 --> 00:12:24.672
the language model and
get back one response.

253
00:12:25.193 --> 00:12:27.334
So it's
quite simple to understand what's going

254
00:12:27.334 --> 00:12:29.742
on. It's quite cheap
and it works pretty well.

255
00:12:30.424 --> 00:12:32.047
But that doesn't always work okay.

256
00:12:32.688 --> 00:12:34.759
So if you remember,
when we fetched the

257
00:12:34.759 --> 00:12:37.347
documents in the notebook,
we only got four documents back

258
00:12:37.347 --> 00:12:38.641
and they were relatively small.

259
00:12:39.282 --> 00:12:42.024
But what if you wanted to do the
same type of question

260
00:12:42.024 --> 00:12:43.851
answering over lots of
different types of chunks?

261
00:12:44.533 --> 00:12:47.178
Then there are a few
different methods that we can use.

262
00:12:47.178 --> 00:12:48.140
The first is "Map_reduce".

263
00:12:48.701 --> 00:12:52.373
This basically takes all the chunks,
passes them along with the

264
00:12:52.373 --> 00:12:56.379
question to a language model,
gets back a response, and then uses

265
00:12:56.379 --> 00:12:59.384
another language model
call to summarize all of the

266
00:12:59.384 --> 00:13:01.387
individual responses into
a final answer.

267
00:13:02.349 --> 00:13:04.618
This is really powerful
because it can operate

268
00:13:04.618 --> 00:13:06.036
over any number of documents.

269
00:13:06.838 --> 00:13:09.515
And it's also really powerful because
you can do the

270
00:13:09.515 --> 00:13:10.586
individual questions in parallel.


271
00:13:11.788 --> 00:13:15.439
But it does take a lot more calls.
And it does treat

272
00:13:15.439 --> 00:13:18.177
all the documents as independent,
which may not always

273
00:13:18.177 --> 00:13:21.219
be the most desired thing. "Refine",
which is another method,

274
00:13:21.219 --> 00:13:23.652
is again used to
loop over many documents.

275
00:13:24.193 --> 00:13:26.879
But it actually does it iteratively.
It builds upon the

276
00:13:26.879 --> 00:13:28.222
answer from the previous document.

277
00:13:28.723 --> 00:13:31.707
So this is really good for
combining information and

278
00:13:31.707 --> 00:13:35.686
building up an answer over time.
It will generally lead to longer

279
00:13:35.686 --> 00:13:36.017
answers.

280
00:13:36.518 --> 00:13:39.909
And it's also not as fast because
now the calls aren't independent.

281
00:13:39.909 --> 00:13:42.170
They depend on the result of
previous calls.

282
00:13:43.312 --> 00:13:45.816
This means that it
often takes a good

283
00:13:45.816 --> 00:13:49.258
while longer and takes
just as many calls as "Map_reduce", basically.

284
00:13:49.258 --> 00:13:52.075
"Map_rerank" is a pretty interesting and
a bit more

285
00:13:52.075 --> 00:13:55.830
experimental one where you do a
single call to the language model

286
00:13:55.830 --> 00:13:59.585
for each document. And you
also ask it to return a score.

287
00:13:59.585 --> 00:13:59.585


288
00:14:00.227 --> 00:14:01.810
And then you select the highest score.

289
00:14:02.551 --> 00:14:04.657
This relies on the
language model to know

290
00:14:04.657 --> 00:14:08.079
what the score should be.
So you often have to tell it, "Hey,

291
00:14:08.079 --> 00:14:11.765
it should be a high score if it's
relevant to the document and really

292
00:14:11.765 --> 00:14:13.871
refine the instructions
there". Similar to "Map_reduce", all

293
00:14:13.871 --> 00:14:15.450
the
calls are independent. So you

294
00:14:15.450 --> 00:14:18.872
can batch them and it's relatively fast.
But again, you're making a bunch

295
00:14:18.872 --> 00:14:20.978
of language model calls.
So it will be

296
00:14:20.978 --> 00:14:22.031
a bit more expensive.


297
00:14:22.692 --> 00:14:25.080
The most common of
these methods is the "stuff method",

298
00:14:25.080 --> 00:14:26.990
which we used in
the notebook to combine

299
00:14:26.990 --> 00:14:28.184
it all into one document.

300
00:14:29.186 --> 00:14:32.502
The second most common is the "Map_reduce"
method, which takes these chunks

301
00:14:32.502 --> 00:14:34.436
and sends them to the language
model.

302
00:14:35.479 --> 00:14:38.532
These methods here, stuff, map_reduce,
refine, and rerank can also

303
00:14:38.532 --> 00:14:41.281
be used for lots of other
chains besides just

304
00:14:41.281 --> 00:14:41.892
question answering.

305
00:14:42.713 --> 00:14:45.832
For example,
a really common use case of the "Map_reduce"

306
00:14:45.832 --> 00:14:49.263
chain is for summarization, where
you have a really long document

307
00:14:49.263 --> 00:14:51.134
and you want
to recursively summarize

308
00:14:51.134 --> 00:14:52.694
pieces of information
in it.

309
00:14:53.615 --> 00:14:55.559
That's it for question
answering over documents.

310
00:14:56.141 --> 00:14:59.266
As you may have noticed, there's
a lot going on in the

311
00:14:59.266 --> 00:15:02.913
different chains that we have here.
And so in the next section, we'll cover

312
00:15:02.913 --> 00:15:04.997
ways to better understand
what exactly is going

313
00:15:04.997 --> 00:15:06.560
on inside all of
these chains.
